WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:14.130
<v Ira Glass>When Aaryn was nine, her family moved from China to Canada. And there was this pool in their apartment complex. And one day her dad was like, let's try out the pool. And Aaryn's super excited and her dad is meanwhile thinking, she doesn't know how to swim. Time to fix that.
<v Ira Glass>当Aaryn九岁时，全家从中国搬到了加拿大。他们公寓大楼里有个游泳池。有一天她爸爸说，去泳池试试吧。Aaryn非常兴奋，而她爸爸心里却在想，她还不会游泳，是时候解决这个问题了。

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.750
<v Ira Glass>And as soon as they get to the pool he picks her up, throws her in the deep end.
<v Ira Glass>他们一到泳池边，他抱起她就扔进了深水区。

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:37.960
<v Aaryn Zhou>And I start screaming. And I still didn't really know what was going on. And I was screaming and struggling in the water. And every few seconds I can kind of see above water my dad standing there like looking at me. And I'm screaming and he's not really reacting.
<v Aaryn Zhou>我开始尖叫。当时我还真不知道发生了什么。我在水里尖叫挣扎，每隔几秒钟，我能从水面上方看到爸爸站在那里看着我。我在尖叫，而他却没什么反应。

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:44.910
<v Aaryn Zhou>And then I see like the other kids and their parents. And they were looking really disapproving.
<v Aaryn Zhou>然后我看到了其他孩子和他们的父母。他们看起来非常不赞同。

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:50.650
<v Ira Glass>Did you feel, like at the moment that he threw you in, did you feel betrayed by him?
<v Ira Glass>在你被扔进去的那一刻，你有感到被他背叛了吗？

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:58.840
<v Aaryn Zhou>I felt angry. I felt angry that I was embarrassed because of something he did.
<v Aaryn Zhou>我感到愤怒。我感到愤怒是因为他的所作所为让我感到尴尬。

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:11.710
<v Ira Glass>Did you feel angry that you might drown? I mean, aren't you leaving out that? I like that the embarrassment is a bigger thing in your head at that moment than like oh, I might actually just die right now.
<v Ira Glass>你有没有因为可能会淹死而感到愤怒？我是说，你是不是忽略了这一点？我倒觉得，那一刻你脑子里尴尬比“天哪，我现在可能会死”更重要。

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:29.280
<v Aaryn Zhou>I really didn't think I would. Like that didn't even crossed my mind. Like what I was thinking the most was oh, my god. All these white kids and their parents are watching me. Like I was trying to will myself into learning how to swim so I wouldn't have to keep embarrassing myself.
<v Aaryn Zhou>我真的没觉得我会死。这念头根本没在我脑子里出现过。我当时想得最多的是，天哪，所有这些白人小孩和他们的父母都在看着我。我当时在拼命想办法学会游泳，这样我就不用再丢脸了。

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:43.210
<v Ira Glass>It is literally only now, only once she started talking about this for our radio show that the thought occurred to her that maybe all those people weren't looking disapprovingly at her, but at her dad. Back when she was nine she just blamed herself for failing.
<v Ira Glass>直到现在，直到她开始为我们的广播节目谈论这件事，她才意识到，也许那些人看着的不是她，而是她爸爸。九岁时，她只是因为自己做不到而自责。

00:01:43.210 --> 00:02:06.140
<v Aaryn Zhou>I remember thinking like how could you not know how to swim? You just have to swim and be OK. Look at all these people staring at you. And look at the look on your dad's face. Just swim.
<v Aaryn Zhou>我记得当时在想，你怎么连游泳都不会？你只要游起来就没事了。看看所有这些人都在盯着你。看看你爸爸脸上的表情。快游啊。

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:28.530
<v Aaryn Zhou>And then I feel myself sinking and I can only see water around me. And then a few seconds later, I don't know how long, I feel someone grab me. And then I see that it's my dad. And we slowly get out of the pool. And he didn't really talk to me for the rest of the night.
<v Aaryn Zhou>然后我感觉到自己在下沉，周围只有水。几秒钟后，我不知道过了多久，我感觉到有人抓住了我。然后我看到是我爸爸。我们慢慢地走出了泳池。那天晚上剩下的时间里他都没跟我说话。

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:37.140
<v Ira Glass>She did overhear him telling her mom, I don't know what's wrong, it should have been easy. She should've been able to do it. In the years since, she has never talked to him about it.
<v Ira Glass>她确实无意中听到他跟她妈妈说，不知道怎么回事，这应该很容易的。她本该能做到的。从那以后的几年里，她从未和他谈起过这件事。

00:02:37.140 --> 00:03:00.610
<v Ira Glass>But this whole idea of sink or swim, I mean, the actual sink or swim-- you know, throw a kid into the deep end of the pool-- it seems so contrary to all of the other nicey, nicey ways that we try to teach kids about everything else. And it is way more common than you think. It happened to two of our producers here on the show, Emmanuel and Matt. It happened to my wife. They were just tossed in over their heads, no warning, totally caught off guard.
<v Ira Glass>但这种“沉还是游”的理念，也就是真正的“沉还是游”——你知道，把孩子扔进深水区——这似乎与我们试图教孩子其他事情的所有那些温和、委婉的方式背道而驰。而且这比你想象的要普遍得多。我们节目的两位制作人Emmanuel和Matt都经历过，我妻子也经历过。他们都是在毫无防备的情况下，突然被扔进了深水里。

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:08.790
<v Ira Glass>Aaryn says it didn't traumatize her. In fact, it motivated her to try to teach herself to swim a month later so there'd be no further embarrassment.
<v Ira Glass>Aaryn说这并没有给她留下心理创伤。事实上，这激励她在一个月后尝试自学游泳，这样就不会再尴尬了。

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.730
<v Ira Glass>You know what's the weird part of this strategy is the element of surprise.
<v Ira Glass>你知道这个策略最奇怪的地方就是突袭的成分。

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.160
<v Aaryn Zhou>Yeah.
<v Aaryn Zhou>是啊。

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:31.980
<v Ira Glass>Like it seems like if you're going to do this with your kid you could say, here's what we're going to do. I'm going to throw you in the pool and you're going to swim your way out. Like the thought that he would surprise you, it just seems like a completely unnecessary extra bonus piece of trauma to throw on the whole thing.
<v Ira Glass>感觉如果你要和孩子这样做的话，你可以说，我们要这样做。我要把你扔进泳池，你要自己游出来。他那种突袭你的想法，似乎完全没必要，只是给整件事额外增加了一点心理创伤。

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.570
<v Aaryn Zhou>Yeah, I don't know why he chose to do that. I should ask him some time.
<v Aaryn Zhou>是啊，我不知道他为什么选择那样做。我哪天应该问问他。

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.790
<v Ira Glass>Can we call him?
<v Ira Glass>我们可以打给他吗？

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:50.440
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE BEEPING]
<v Ira Glass>[电话嘟嘟声]

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.010
<v Mom>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Mom>[非英语语音]

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.840
<v Aaryn Zhou>Mama.
<v Aaryn Zhou>妈妈。

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.220
<v Mom>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Mom>[非英语语音]

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.080
<v Aaryn Zhou>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Aaryn Zhou>[非英语语音]

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:06.830
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is the universal sound of one parent getting the other parent on the phone. Once her dad was on the line, Aaryn explained why we were calling.
<v Ira Glass>这当然是全世界通用的父母给另一方打电话的声音。Aaryn的爸爸接通电话后，Aaryn解释了我们为什么要打这个电话。

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:07.300
<v Aaryn Zhou>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] Deep end.
<v Aaryn Zhou>[非英语语音] 深水区。

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:21.230
<v Ira Glass>And I have to say, this must have been a very strange call for Aaryn's dad to get at 10:00 at night in China. His daughter lives in Europe these days, plus some man from a radio show in America he's never heard of asking about a totally random incident that happened in Canada 16 years ago.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，对于身在中国、晚上十点接到这个电话的Aaryn的爸爸来说，这一定非常奇怪。他的女儿如今住在欧洲，还有一个他从未听说过的美国广播节目的男人，问起16年前在加拿大发生的一件完全随机的琐事。

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:29.550
<v Dad>I cannot exactly remember that. That's really a long time ago. I cannot remember it.
<v Dad>我不记得具体情况了。那是很久以前的事了。我不记得了。

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:45.520
<v Ira Glass>The phone line is bad. He's saying I cannot exactly remember that. That's really a long time ago. I cannot remember it. Aaryn's dad said that being thrown into the deep end of the pool was not like some traditional way they taught swimming back home in the village he came from. He didn't learn that way.
<v Ira Glass>电话线路不好。他说我不记得具体情况了。那是很久以前的事了。我不记得了。Aaryn的爸爸说，被扔进深水区并不是他们老家那种教游泳的传统方式。他自己也不是那样学的。

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:53.060
<v Ira Glass>And yet when I asked him would he teach a child today to swim that way, would he throw her into the deep end of the pool? He said yeah, maybe.
<v Ira Glass>然而，当我问他今天是否还会用那种方式教孩子游泳，是否会把她扔进深水区时，他说，也许吧。

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.090
<v Dad>Maybe. I think maybe.
<v Dad>也许。我觉得也许吧。

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.500
<v Aaryn Zhou>Maybe.
<v Aaryn Zhou>也许。

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.520
<v Ira Glass>So you don't think it's a bad idea?
<v Ira Glass>所以你不觉得这是个坏主意？

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.330
<v Dad>No, I don't think so. No.
<v Dad>不，我不这么认为。不。

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:17.060
<v Ira Glass>Aaryn was unsurprised. When he got off the phone she told me even when she was nine and flailing around in the water, for him to throw her in like that, it wasn't like she was seeing some insane side of him she'd never seen before, some unheard of behavior.
<v Ira Glass>Aaryn并不感到惊讶。当她挂断电话后，她告诉我，即使在她九岁时在水里乱扑腾的时候，他那样把她扔进去，对她来说也并不像是看到了他从未展现过的疯狂一面，或者什么闻所未闻的行为。

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:28.330
<v Aaryn Zhou>No, that's not insane for my dad. Like it seemed really on character for him to do that. Like if it had been anything else, driving a car.
<v Aaryn Zhou>不，对我爸爸来说这并不疯狂。这对他来说真的很符合他的性格。如果是其他事情的话，比如开车。

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:49.260
<v Aaryn Zhou>Like with roller blades he just dropped me off at a grocery store parking lot, made me put them on myself, and then gave me a shopping cart and said hold on to this and you'll learn. And then he drove away and said come home when you're tired. It's just how he thinks I'll learn best, I guess.
<v Aaryn Zhou>就像轮滑，他只是把我扔在杂货店的停车场，让我自己穿上，然后给了我一个购物车，说抓紧它，你就会学会了。然后他就开车走了，说累了就回家。我想这就是他认为我能学得最好的方式吧。

00:05:49.260 --> 00:06:11.990
<v Ira Glass>This is a whole coherent strategy for life. It's tough out there. Stuff's gotta get done. Coddling ourselves, coddling others, that's not going to do anybody any good. Today on our program we have two very different stories in which people are thrown into the deep end of the pool, asked to do things they were totally inexperienced at and unprepared for in situations with very high stakes.
<v Ira Glass>这是一种完整的人生策略。外面的世界很艰难。事情必须完成。溺爱自己，溺爱别人，对谁都没有好处。今天我们的节目有两个非常不同的故事，人们都被扔进了深水区，被要求去做他们完全没有经验、没有准备的事，处境非常高风险。

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:39.990
<v Ira Glass>In one story, a man faces 20 years in prison. And in the other, the United States faces an implacable foe on a quest for global domination. I am not kidding, global domination. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>在一个故事中，一名男子面临20年监禁。在另一个故事中，美国面临着一个为了全球统治而不懈追求的敌人。我没开玩笑，全球统治。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:45.630
<v Ira Glass>Act One, If You Cannot Afford an Attorney, Some Random Dude Will Be Appointed to You.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，如果你请不起律师，会有一个随机分配的家伙。

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:58.850
<v Ira Glass>So one of the many, many things that we all know from watching television is that when you get arrested, someone is supposed to read you your Miranda rights. And those rights include the right to an attorney. And if you cannot afford an attorney, one will be given to you for free.
<v Ira Glass>我们从电视节目中都知道的一件事是，当你被捕时，有人应该宣读你的米兰达权利。这些权利包括聘请律师的权利。如果你请不起律师，政府会免费为你提供一名律师。

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:22.270
<v Ira Glass>Well, those free attorneys, public defenders, they have never been adequately funded in lots of states. And in recent years, the problem has reached crisis levels. Lots of public defenders offices around the country simply do not have the money and staff to provide a lawyer to everyone who needs one. And in some states, Florida, Tennessee, Missouri, Montana, Louisiana, the court just picks random lawyers and makes them take the cases.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那些免费律师，即公设辩护人，在许多州从未获得足够的资助。近年来，这个问题已经达到了危机水平。全国各地的许多公设辩护人办公室根本没有足够的资金和人员为每个需要的人提供律师。在一些州，如佛罗里达州、田纳西州、密苏里州、蒙大拿州、路易斯安那州，法院只是随机挑选律师，并强迫他们接手这些案件。

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:43.560
<v Ira Glass>A few weeks ago you might have seen this in the news, the public defender's office in Missouri tried to assign a case like this, it was an assault case, to the governor of Missouri, Jay Nixon, who's a member of the bar. The head of the public defender's office said it was appropriate to give the case to him because quote, "he was the one attorney in the state who not only created this problem, but is in a unique position to address it" by giving them more funding.
<v Ira Glass>几周前你可能在新闻中看到过，密苏里州的公设辩护人办公室试图将一个案件，这是一个袭击案，分配给密苏里州州长Jay Nixon，他也是律师协会的成员。公设辩护人办公室负责人说，把案件交给他很合适，因为“他是州里唯一不仅制造了这个问题，而且处于独特位置来解决它的人”，即给他们更多资助。

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:58.890
<v Ira Glass>The governor, apparently, is not going to take that case. But other lawyers have to. And there's an interesting problem when they assign these random lawyers to these cases. Often, these attorneys have no experience at all in the kind of law they're going to have to use in the cases.
<v Ira Glass>州长显然不会接那个案子。但其他律师必须接。当他们将这些随机律师分配到这些案件时，出现了一个有趣的问题。通常，这些律师在他们必须处理的案件类型上完全没有经验。

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:12.190
<v Ira Glass>So for example, if you're picked up for marijuana possession, your fate might be in the hands of a lawyer who specializes in real estate. Reporter David Zax has a story of a case like that in Louisiana, Shreveport, which is in Caddo Parish.
<v Ira Glass>例如，如果你因为持有大麻被捕，你的命运可能掌握在一个专门从事房地产工作的律师手中。记者David Zax有关于路易斯安那州什里夫波特（卡多教区）此类案件的故事。

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:32.640
<v Ira Glass>The case went to a guy who is not prepared for it. Quick warning to listeners, there is no explicit or graphic content in this story at all, but there is some colorful cursing. That's actually true for both acts of today's program. And if you prefer a show that has been beeped, maybe you listen with kids in the car, you can get that at our website thisamericanlife.org. OK, here's David.
<v Ira Glass>案件交给了一个对此毫无准备的家伙。提醒听众，这个故事中没有任何露骨或血腥的内容，但有一些粗鲁的咒骂。今天节目的两个部分其实都是如此。如果你喜欢经过消音处理的节目，或者如果你和孩子们一起在车里听，你可以在我们的网站thisamericanlife.org上找到。好了，听听David的讲述。

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:42.840
<v David Zax>This case is about a burglary. One night a few years ago, a woman woke up to a flashlight shining in her face. This was in Shreveport, Louisiana, in the northwest corner of the state.
<v David Zax>这个案件是关于入室盗窃的。几年前的一个晚上，一位女士醒来时发现手电筒照在她的脸上。这发生在路易斯安那州什里夫波特市，该州的西北角。

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:57.110
<v David Zax>The woman screamed and the burglars fled. They made off with two TVs, a computer, an iPhone. But they'd also left some evidence behind, a cigarette butt, a crowbar, and a hat, a black baseball cap.
<v David Zax>女士尖叫起来，盗贼逃跑了。他们偷走了两台电视、一台电脑、一部iPhone。但他们也留下了一些证据，一个烟蒂、一根撬棍和一顶帽子，一顶黑色棒球帽。

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:15.350
<v David Zax>The police bagged the hat and they sent it to the crime lab for DNA testing. It turned out that some DNA in the hat matched a man named Trastavien Hardy. A warrant was issued for his arrest and he turned himself in. Shreveport was one of those places assigning private attorneys to cases like this because they don't have enough public defenders.
<v David Zax>警方把帽子装进证物袋，送往犯罪实验室进行DNA检测。结果发现，帽子上的DNA与一名叫Trastavien Hardy的男子相符。针对他的逮捕令发布了，他自首了。什里夫波特就是那些将此类案件分配给私人律师的地方，因为他们没有足够的公设辩护人。

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:27.050
<v David Zax>So the judge, he looked at this list he had of private attorneys in the area. It was alphabetical, and he just picked the next name. Jack Bailey, one of those accident and injury guys whose ads you see all over the place.
<v David Zax>所以法官看着他手里的当地私人律师名单。那是按字母顺序排列的，他选了下一个名字。Jack Bailey，那些你在到处都能看到广告的事故和伤害律师之一。

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:37.130
<v Jack Bailey>I hope you work every day without being hurt on the job, drive without someone running a stop sign, go through life without ever being hurt because of someone else's stupidity.
<v Jack Bailey>我希望你每天工作都不会受伤，开车时不会有人闯红灯，一生中都不会因为别人的愚蠢而受伤。

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:44.480
<v David Zax>This one is beautifully lit and shot. Jack is sitting at a big desk. He's in shirt sleeves and suspenders looking quietly tough.
<v David Zax>这一个拍得灯光亮丽。Jack坐在大桌子旁。他穿着衬衫，戴着背带，看起来沉稳而强硬。

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:55.690
<v Jack Bailey>But bad things happen to good people. And if it should happen to you, you're going to want someone like me by your side. And around here, there's only one someone like me.
<v Jack Bailey>但坏事总会发生在好人身上。如果你遇到了这种情况，你会想要像我这样的人在你身边。而在这一带，像我这样的人只有一个。

00:09:55.690 --> 00:10:16.750
<v David Zax>Jack Bailey is 64. And for over 30 years, he's made his money on workplace accidents, car crashes, medical malpractice, that kind of thing. Jack likes to call himself a podunk shirtsleeve lawyer. But he's been really successful. He wears monogrammed shirts under those suspenders. He's got a huge stately office with bayonets and a Don't Tread on Me flag on the wall.
<v David Zax>Jack Bailey 64岁了。30多年来，他靠处理工作事故、车祸、医疗事故等这类事情赚钱。Jack喜欢称自己为乡下的便装律师。但他非常成功。他在背带下面穿的是绣有名字缩写的衬衫。他有一间豪华的办公室，墙上挂着刺刀和“别踩我”的旗帜。

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.590
<v David Zax>He's got a showy dog too, a bull terrier that hangs out with him all day.
<v David Zax>他还有一只引人注目的狗，一只终日陪着他的牛头梗。

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:27.820
<v Jack Bailey>Oh yeah, he's friendly. Hey Tater, somebody wants to meet you. Tater, wake up.
<v Jack Bailey>噢，是的，他很友好。嘿Tater，有人想见你。Tater，醒醒。

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:41.070
<v David Zax>Jack's always liked doing this kind of work. He gets to pick his clients, make his own schedule. He finds out he's been assigned Trastavien's case in April 2015 when a fax comes in, a single-page court order. Jack picks it up, looks at it.
<v David Zax>Jack一直喜欢做这种工作。他可以挑选客户，自己安排时间。2015年4月，当传真传来一张单页法院命令时，他发现自己被分配处理Trastavien的案子。Jack拿起它，看了一眼。

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:45.850
<v Tammy Burdine>And he's very unhappy, and his face was kind of red.
<v Tammy Burdine>他非常不高兴，脸都涨红了。

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:53.810
<v David Zax>This is Tammy Burdine. She's been Jack's paralegal for over a decade. Jack took the fax to his office, made a racket. He was yelling to himself.
<v David Zax>这是Tammy Burdine。她担任Jack的律师助理已经十多年了。Jack把传真带回办公室，闹得鸡飞狗跳。他在对自己大吼大叫。

00:10:53.810 --> 00:11:07.690
<v Tammy Burdine>He was in his office for a little while and he came out and he made the comment to me, well, it looks like we're representing a damn criminal.
<v Tammy Burdine>他在办公室待了一会儿，出来后对我说了句，好吧，看来我们得为一个该死的罪犯辩护了。

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:12.310
<v David Zax>The court order was a form letter. Jack's name was scribbled into a blank. He still has it.
<v David Zax>法院命令是一份格式信。Jack的名字被草率地写在一个空白处。他现在还留着它。

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:39.190
<v Jack Bailey>Therefore, it is ordered that Attorney Jack Bailey be appointed to represent the defendant in this matter. By the way, it doesn't say this, but for free at my cost. Because it cost hundreds of dollars an hour to operate my office. So I'm actually paying to represent somebody. This isn't free.
<v Jack Bailey>因此，命令律师Jack Bailey被指派代理本案被告。顺便说一下，虽然上面没写，但这得由我自费。因为运营我的办公室每小时要花几百美元。所以我实际上是在花钱去为某人辩护。这可不是免费的。

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:55.080
<v David Zax>Tammy was doing her best not to panic over how she and Jack were going to handle the case they'd been assigned. Because Jack doesn't do criminal cases. He doesn't deal with people facing prison time. But Tammy knew that Jack had at least dabbled in criminal cases for a few months early in his career. Maybe they'd be OK.
<v David Zax>Tammy尽力不因她和Jack将如何处理被指派的案件而惊慌。因为Jack不处理刑事案件。他不接触面临监禁的人。但Tammy知道Jack在职业生涯早期至少在几个月内涉足过刑事案件。也许他们会没事的。

00:11:55.080 --> 00:12:04.010
<v Tammy Burdine>Surely law school taught him stuff about criminals. Surely somebody taught them something about criminal.
<v Tammy Burdine>法律学校肯定教过他关于罪犯的事情。肯定有人教过他们一些刑事方面的知识。

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:10.070
<v Jack Bailey>I hadn't read the Louisiana Code of Criminal Procedure in over 30 years when I got this appointment.
<v Jack Bailey>当我接到这个指派时，我已经30多年没读过路易斯安那州刑事诉讼法典了。

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:17.630
<v Tammy Burdine>I guess I just thought, well, Mr. Bailey has practiced long enough. He's a lawyer. He knows what to do.
<v Tammy Burdine>我想我只是认为，好吧，Bailey先生执业够久了。他是个律师。他知道该怎么做。

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:29.000
<v Jack Bailey>I'm upset. I'm worried about whether I'm going to be able to do a good job or not. I'm worried about how much time I'm gonna have to invest in it. There is nothing good to say about it.
<v Jack Bailey>我很沮丧。我很担心自己能否做得好。我担心我得投入多少时间。这件事没一点好处。

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:35.600
<v David Zax>So Jack came up with a plan. He thought the appointment had to be illegal. So he told Tammy, get me out of this.
<v David Zax>所以Jack想出了一个计划。他认为这个指派一定是违法的。所以他告诉Tammy，帮我弄掉这个。

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:47.960
<v David Zax>She started drafting letters of complaint to send off to the judge and to the public defenders. But because she was worried they might get stuck with the case, on her own, she started doing something else. She started working it.
<v David Zax>她开始起草投诉信，准备寄给法官和公设辩护人。但因为担心会被这个案子缠住，她自作主张做了另一件事。她开始着手处理案子。

00:12:47.960 --> 00:13:03.720
<v David Zax>Tammy is super organized. Wire-rimmed glasses, late 40s, a smoker. And because Jack hasn't told her to do this work, she has to hide her tracks. When she wants to call the clerk of court to ask a question she makes sure she's out of earshot of Jack's office. Her desk is right outside his door.
<v David Zax>Tammy非常专业。戴着金属框眼镜，40多岁，是个烟民。因为Jack没让她做这些工作，她必须隐藏踪迹。当她想给法庭书记员打电话问问题时，她会确保避开Jack办公室的听力范围。她的办公桌就在他的门外。

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:20.250
<v Tammy Burdine>He would come out of his office and I'm minimizing WordPerfect. And I'm on Caddo's website. And I hear, you can hear his chair go creak. And I'm like, oh, crap. Here he comes. He's up, he's up. And I'm closing things.
<v Tammy Burdine>他会从办公室出来，而我正在最小化WordPerfect。我正开着Caddo的网站。然后我听到，你可以听到他的椅子发出吱呀声。我在想，噢，糟糕。他来了。他站起来了，他站起来了。我在关掉窗口。

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:46.610
<v Tammy Burdine>And I'm trying my best. I don't want to poke-- we laugh about it here, but the rule is you don't poke the bear. And I am worried about him seeing things on my computer poking the bear. But I have to, but I was told he didn't want us to. And I'm so confused on this whole case on every level.
<v Tammy Burdine>我在尽力而为。我不想惹——我们在这里拿这个开玩笑，但规则是你不能惹火烧身。我担心他看到我电脑上的东西，那是在招惹麻烦。但我不得不做，虽然我被告知他不想让我们处理。在这个案子的各个层面上我都感到非常困惑。

00:13:46.610 --> 00:14:05.700
<v David Zax>She started where any of us would, Google. First she looks at the basics of criminal procedure in Louisiana and also the basics of her own case. The state had given them almost nothing. On the court order there wasn't even a charge listed, just a docket number and the guy's name, Trastavien Hardy.
<v David Zax>她从任何一个人都会用的工具开始，谷歌。首先她查看了路易斯安那州刑事诉讼的基础知识，以及她自己案子的基础知识。州政府几乎什么都没给他们。在法院命令上甚至没写指控，只有一个案卷号和那个人的名字，Trastavien Hardy。

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:19.030
<v David Zax>In her googling, Tammy finds a mug shot and a rap sheet. Trastavien is 25 and his record goes back years. There is a marijuana possession, a car break in, and he's a registered sex offender. But that's all she can find.
<v David Zax>在谷歌搜索中，Tammy找到了入狱照和前科单。Trastavien 25岁，他的记录可以追溯到几年前。有持有大麻、闯入汽车记录，他还是注册性犯罪者。但那就是她能找到的所有信息了。

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:23.920
<v David Zax>So she just waits. She assumes that the DA's office or the court is going to send more information.
<v David Zax>所以她只是等着。她认为地方检察官办公室或法院会寄来更多信息。

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:50.490
<v Tammy Burdine>I mean, you know, if you watch the TV shows, the Law & Order , the Criminal Minds , they have evidence. We have evidence in the civil matters. So I assumed the rest of it was coming, maybe in a box or a big envelope. And it never came. It just didn't.
<v Tammy Burdine>我是说，你知道，如果你看电视节目，比如《法律与秩序》、《犯罪心理》，他们会有证据。我们在民事案件中有证据。所以我以为其余的资料会寄过来，也许放在盒子里或者大信封里。但它从来没来。就是没来。

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.970
<v David Zax>A month passed. Then on May 13--
<v David Zax>一个月过去了。然后是5月13日——

00:14:53.970 --> 00:15:22.240
<v Tammy Burdine>The phone rings and the receptionist answers it and he transfers the call and says Mr. Bailey, the court is on the phone. So he picks it up, this is Jack Bailey. And I can hear Mr. Bailey from where I sit. And he is telling them nobody told us about this date. And nobody said, and I wasn't aware. And you know, we'd missed the date.
<v Tammy Burdine>电话响了，前台接了电话并转接过来，说Bailey先生，法院来电话了。他接起来，说我是Jack Bailey。我从坐的地方能听到Bailey先生的声音。他在告诉对方没人告诉我们这个日期。没人说，我也不知道。你知道，我们错过了那个日期。

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:28.890
<v David Zax>They'd missed a court date on Trastavien's case. Tammy took a closer look at the faxed court order. There it was.
<v David Zax>他们错过了Trastavien案子的出庭日期。Tammy仔细看了那份传真法院命令。上面确实写着。

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:41.180
<v Tammy Burdine>It actually is just handwritten at the bottom of the order and it looks like 5.13. And I know it says court date, but it is bad handwriting.
<v Tammy Burdine>它其实只是手写在命令底部，看起来像是5.13。我知道它写着出庭日期，但这字迹太烂了。

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:46.660
<v David Zax>Of course, there was one person who managed to show up in court that day. He was brought there directly from jail.
<v David Zax>当然，有一个人那天成功出庭了。他被直接从监狱带到了那里。

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:50.560
<v Trastavien Hardy>At that time, I really was just lost.
<v Trastavien Hardy>那时，我真的很迷茫。

00:15:50.560 --> 00:16:02.350
<v David Zax>Jack's client, Trastavien Hardy. He'd come to the courtroom in shackles hoping to finally meet his lawyer. But a bailiff in the courtroom told Trastavien that he hadn't seen Jack at all. Lots of these appointed attorneys weren't showing up.
<v David Zax>Jack的客户，Trastavien Hardy。他戴着镣铐来到法庭，希望能终于见到他的律师。但法庭的一名法警告诉Trastavien，他根本没看到Jack。很多这些被指派的律师都没出现。

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:20.110
<v Trastavien Hardy>You know, I asked him like, where my lawyer at, who my lawyer is. And he  like, you probably never going to get to see him. He'll never really come to court. That's all he just said. Like you'll never get to see him in court. I'm like, dang.
<v Trastavien Hardy>你知道，我问他，我的律师在哪，我的律师是谁。他说，你可能永远也见不到他。他永远不会真的出庭的。他只说了这些。就像你在法庭上永远见不到他一样。我在想，该死。

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:43.970
<v David Zax>Trastavien was eager to see an attorney because he says he hadn't done the burglary. It's true he used to be mixed up in some bad stuff. He'd pled guilty to those things Tammy had read about, though they weren't all as bad as she'd imagined. That registered sex offense, for example, it was consensual. Trastavien was 17, his girlfriend was 14, which at the time was a felony in Louisiana. He served time for it.
<v David Zax>Trastavien渴望见到律师，因为他说他没有入室行窃。没错，他过去确实卷入过一些坏事。他曾对Tammy读到的那些事情认罪，尽管它们并不像她想象的那么严重。例如那个注册性犯罪，那是双方自愿的。Trastavien 17岁，他女朋友14岁，这在当时的路易斯安那州是重罪。他为此服刑了。

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:49.830
<v David Zax>Trastavien is slight and soft spoken. Says yes sir a lot. You have to lean in a bit to hear him sometimes.
<v David Zax>Trastavien瘦小且说话温和。经常说“是的，先生”。你有时必须凑近一点才能听清他的话。

00:16:49.830 --> 00:17:08.730
<v David Zax>And he says that since he got out of prison in 2011, he'd been keeping his nose clean. He was 25. He was a full-time student at a local college. He had just started dating someone, a nurse. He'd been sitting in jail since April terrified, upset, confused. He wanted to get back to his school, to his girlfriend.
<v David Zax>他说自从2011年出狱后，他就一直安分守己。他25岁了。他是当地一所大学的全日制学生。他刚开始和人约会，是一个护士。他从4月起就一直待在监狱里，感到惊恐、沮丧和困惑。他想回到学校，回到他女朋友身边。

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:27.010
<v David Zax>At his first court date he found out that the Caddo public defender's office couldn't offer him a lawyer, so he went back to his cell, waited for a month. Then on May 13, he went to court and his new lawyer, some guy named Jack Bailey, didn't even bother to show.
<v David Zax>在第一次出庭时，他发现卡多公设辩护人办公室无法为他提供律师，所以他回到牢房，等了一个月。然后在5月13日，他出庭了，他的新律师，一个叫Jack Bailey的家伙，甚至懒得露面。

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:32.440
<v David Zax>Back in his cell, Trastavien sat down and wrote a letter to Alan Golden, the chief public defender in Caddo.
<v David Zax>回到牢房里，Trastavien坐下来写了一封信给卡多首席公设辩护人Alan Golden。

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:40.600
<v Trastavien Hardy>Dear Mr. Alan Golden, I was told you were the person to contact as far as reassigning me another public defender.
<v Trastavien Hardy>尊敬的Alan Golden先生，有人告诉我你是负责为我重新分配另一位公设辩护人的联系人。

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:46.350
<v David Zax>Trastavien noted that Jack Bailey had ditched his court date, never tried to contact him, never visited him in jail.
<v David Zax>Trastavien指出Jack Bailey错过了他的出庭日期，从未试图联系他，从未在监狱探视过他。

00:17:46.350 --> 00:18:00.390
<v Trastavien Hardy>So I would like to fire him and be appointed someone who is going to fight for me. Thanks for your time, and I hope this letter don't go unnoticed. Sincerely, Trastavien Hardy.
<v Trastavien Hardy>所以我想要解雇他，并被指派一位会为我而战的人。感谢你的时间，希望这封信不会被忽视。诚挚地，Trastavien Hardy。

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:08.580
<v David Zax>There's one word here, it looks like you went over it several times with the pen. It's pretty thick there. What's the word there?
<v David Zax>这里有一个词，看起来你用笔反复划过了好几次。那里挺厚的。那是什么词？

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:14.040
<v Trastavien Hardy>Fire. Fire.
<v Trastavien Hardy>解雇。解雇。

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:34.400
<v David Zax>Trastavien couldn't fire Jack, of course. There wasn't a backup lawyer to assign to him. Meanwhile, Jack had been sending off his own letters trying to get removed from the case. And by June, he and Tammy realized it wasn't going to work. Jack was stuck with Trastavien, just like Trastavien was stuck with him.
<v David Zax>当然，Trastavien没法解雇Jack。没有后备律师可以指派给他。与此同时，Jack也一直在寄出他自己的信件试图退出案件。到了6月，他和Tammy意识到这行不通。Jack被Trastavien缠住了，就像Trastavien被Jack缠住一样。

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:51.490
<v David Zax>First step, now that Jack was on board, he needed to visit Trastavien. But in order to get into the jail the warden said that he need a bar card, some kind of photo ID that the Louisiana State Bar Association issues. Jack had never needed a bar card in his civil practice. He was fuming.
<v David Zax>第一步，现在Jack已经上船了，他需要去探望Trastavien。但为了进入监狱，监狱长说他需要律师执业证，某种路易斯安那州律师协会颁发的带照片的证件。Jack在民事业务中从未需要过律师执业证。他怒火中烧。

00:18:51.490 --> 00:19:05.170
<v Jack Bailey>Yeah, listen, I'm sort of-- don't take this wrong way, but I'm kind of a big fish in a small pond. I've been here 40 years. I'm in the phone book. I was on the spine of the book. I periodically run TV advertisements.
<v Jack Bailey>是的，听着，我有点——别误会，我算是在小池塘里的一条大鱼。我在这儿40年了。我在电话簿里。我就在书的侧边。我定期投放电视广告。

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:30.580
<v Jack Bailey>I mean, everybody knows me. The sheriff lives down the street from my mama on Moss Point Road. The sheriff has known me for 30 years. And they're telling me I gotta get a bar code to come in as an appointed lawyer to see some poor bastard that's locked up in the jail? I mean, really, theater of the absurd.
<v Jack Bailey>我是说，每个人都知道我。治安官就住在Moss Point路的妈妈家附近。治安官认识我30年了。他们却告诉我，我得弄个律师执业证才能进去作为指派律师去见一个被关在监狱里的可怜混蛋？我是说，真的，荒诞剧。

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:43.080
<v David Zax>They wasted two weeks to get the whole bar card thing sorted out. And then finally, almost two months after getting his appointment, Jack was going to meet his client. So Jack is sitting in the visiting room waiting for Trastavien.
<v David Zax>他们浪费了两周时间才把律师执业证的事情搞定。最后，在得到指派近两个月后，Jack终于要去见他的客户了。所以Jack坐在探视室里等着Trastavien。

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:50.000
<v Jack Bailey>He comes in, I introduce myself. He doesn't know who I am. I have to explain to him who I am, what I'm doing there.
<v Jack Bailey>他进来了，我介绍了自己。他不知道我是谁。我得向他解释我是谁，我在那里做什么。

00:19:50.000 --> 00:20:11.120
<v Jack Bailey>So then he starts telling me, I need to get out of jail. I need to get out of jail. I've lost my job. I've had to drop out of school. Well, the first thing I'm telling him is whoa, slow up, man. You know, tell me about what happened. Are you guilty of this or not?
<v Jack Bailey>然后他开始告诉我，我需要出狱。我需要出狱。我丢了工作。我不得不退学。好吧，我告诉他的第一件事就是，哇，慢点，伙计。你知道，告诉我发生了什么事。你真的犯了这个罪吗？

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:30.460
<v David Zax>Trastavien said he wasn't guilty. He said he didn't know anything about the burglary. But Jack pointed to the warrant which said Trastavien's DNA had been collected from a hat. Trastavien explained that he'd had lots of hats over the years. He'd collected hats. He'd lost hats. He'd traded hats. Even if his DNA was on a hat at the scene, it wasn't proof he'd been there. Jack believed him.
<v David Zax>Trastavien说他没犯罪。他说他对入室盗窃一无所知。但Jack指着逮捕令，上面写着Trastavien的DNA是从一顶帽子上采集的。Trastavien解释说他多年来有很多帽子。他收集帽子。他丢过帽子。他交易过帽子。即使他的DNA在现场的帽子上，也不是他去过那里的证据。Jack相信他。

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:43.510
<v Jack Bailey>You know, look, if you're a good trial lawyer you can detect the BS, right? OK. I mean, you get pretty good at it. I just walked out of there and my gut was telling me this kid wasn't guilty of what he was charged with.
<v Jack Bailey>你知道，听着，如果你是一个好的庭审律师，你可以察觉到谎言，对吧？好吧。我是说，你在这方面相当在行。我刚从那里走出来，直觉告诉我这个孩子并没有犯他被指控的罪行。

00:20:43.510 --> 00:21:06.000
<v Jack Bailey>Also, if he had done this burglary, he would have had more information to give me. I don't find that clients are shy about sharing information with their lawyer, OK. So it's not like the other conversations I had in my career with criminal defendants that were guilty.
<v Jack Bailey>另外，如果他真的做了这次入室盗窃，他会提供更多信息给我。我发现客户在和他们的律师分享信息时并不害羞，好吧。所以这和我职业生涯中遇到的其他有罪的刑事被告的对话不一样。

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:19.590
<v Jack Bailey>Because let me tell you, they all know what's going on. And they all ask you, well, does the state know about this? What witnesses do they have? Did they find this? And Mr. Hardy knew precious little about this.
<v Jack Bailey>因为让我告诉你，他们都知道发生了什么。他们都会问你，好吧，政府知道这个吗？他们有什么证人？他们找到了这个吗？而Hardy先生对这些了解甚少。

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:31.990
<v David Zax>Jack was suddenly invested in the case. Up till now he'd assumed Trastavien was probably guilty. In his experience, that's true of most people accused of crimes. Around the office, Jack had been calling Trastavien "our criminal."
<v David Zax>Jack突然对这个案子投入进去了。直到现在，他一直认为Trastavien可能是有罪的。根据他的经验，大多数被指控犯罪的人确实如此。在办公室里，Jack一直称Trastavien为“我们的罪犯”。

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:53.760
<v David Zax>But now he was ready to fight for him, go to trial. Which presented a problem. His client didn't want to do that. Unlike Jack, Trastavien did have some recent experience in the criminal justice system. He'd stood in front of a lot of judges. He'd spent three years in prison. And he knew most everybody takes a plea. Nobody goes to trial. He told Jack--
<v David Zax>但现在他准备为他而战，进行审判。这带来了一个问题。他的客户不想那样做。不像Jack，Trastavien确实在刑事司法系统中有一些近期的经验。他站在很多法官面前过。他在监狱里待了三年。他知道大多数人都选择了认罪协议。没人会去进行审判。他告诉Jack——

00:21:53.760 --> 00:22:05.210
<v Trastavien Hardy>I'll just plead guilty to anything within a year to go home. I'm like, I just want to go home. I could take a year of something, two years of probation.
<v Trastavien Hardy>我宁愿就任何事情认罪，只要能在一年内回家。我就想回家。我可以接受一年的刑期，或者两年缓刑。

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:11.970
<v David Zax>So you were saying you were ready to plead guilty to just about anything if you could get out in a year. I mean, you've kind of--
<v David Zax>所以你的意思是，只要能在一年内出狱，你就愿意就任何事情认罪。我是说，你已经有点——

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:16.760
<v Trastavien Hardy>Misdemeanor, that's what I was really trying to just get a misdemeanor.
<v Trastavien Hardy>轻罪，我只是想争取一个轻罪。

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.900
<v David Zax>But Jack was not hearing that. He wanted a trial.
<v David Zax>但Jack没听这个。他想要审判。

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:24.390
<v David Zax>Did Jack explain to you that he was not a criminal defense attorney?
<v David Zax>Jack有没有向你解释过他不是刑事辩护律师？

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:29.330
<v Trastavien Hardy>Oh, yes, sir. He had let me know that from the beginning.
<v Trastavien Hardy>哦，有的，先生。他从一开始就让我知道了。

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:35.050
<v David Zax>How did you react when you heard this was his first criminal case in like 30 years?
<v David Zax>当你听说这是他30年来第一个刑事案件时，你有什么反应？

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:51.100
<v Trastavien Hardy>I really was like, after I just really sat down and talked to him I was like at peace. Like, I know I'm in here. I know somebody, somebody came to see me. I know somebody that wants to help me.
<v Trastavien Hardy>我真的，在我坐下来和他真正交谈后，我感到平静。就像，我知道我在这里。我知道有人，有人来看我了。我知道有人想要帮助我。

00:22:51.100 --> 00:23:02.220
<v David Zax>So now Jack had to prepare a case. Luckily, Tammy had been doing all that secret work on the case behind Jack's back. Or not so secret, it turns out. Turns out the bear knows what's going on in his woods.
<v David Zax>所以现在Jack必须准备案子。幸运的是，Tammy一直在Jack背后秘密处理这个案子。或者说并不那么秘密，事实证明。事实证明熊知道它的森林里发生了什么。

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:08.610
<v Jack Bailey>Well yeah, I walk right by her desk all the time. I see what's up on her computer screen.
<v Jack Bailey>嗯，是的，我一直走过她的桌子。我能看到她电脑屏幕上的内容。

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:28.430
<v David Zax>By the time they got to trial, Tammy worked up a master timeline, a list of evidence, a list of evidence and discovery not produced, questions to use in cross-examination, and 126 pages of details on other possible suspects.
<v David Zax>当他们进入审判时，Tammy整理出了一份主时间表、一份证据清单、一份未提供的证据和开示清单、交叉询问的问题，以及126页关于其他可能嫌疑人的详细信息。

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:47.940
<v David Zax>June 22, 2015, on this date, Jack and Trastavien had their first preliminary examination. Basically, it was a pre-trial court date where the DA was supposed to outline their case against Trastavien and prove why he should still be held in jail. Now remember, Jack and Trastavien were going to walk into court and plead not guilty. That was the plan.
<v David Zax>2015年6月22日，在这一天，Jack和Trastavien进行了第一次初步审查。基本上，这是一个审前庭日期，地区检察官应该在这里概述针对Trastavien的案件，并证明为什么他应该继续被关在监狱里。现在记住，Jack和Trastavien要走进法庭并拒绝认罪。那是计划。

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:50.620
<v David Zax>But in court that day, Trastavien got cold feet.
<v David Zax>但在那天法庭上，Trastavien退缩了。

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:57.850
<v Jack Bailey>When he walks in and he comes up to the counsel table, when they brought him down from the jail. First thing he said to me was--
<v Jack Bailey>当他走进来，来到律师桌前，当他们把他从监狱带下来的时候。他对我说的第一件事就是——

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:00.120
<v Trastavien Hardy>I plead guilty right now.
<v Trastavien Hardy>我现在就认罪。

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:03.820
<v David Zax>That is, cop a plea, take a deal, no trial.
<v David Zax>也就是，达成协议，接受交易，不进行审判。

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:11.950
<v Jack Bailey>I said no, you're not doing that. And he goes, but I want to. And I said shut up. Just trust me for a minute.
<v Jack Bailey>我说不，你不会那样做的。他说，但我想要。我说闭嘴。先信任我一下。

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:22.500
<v Trastavien Hardy>His confidence, it's how he talk with that baritone voice, you can't help but be like, well yes, sir. I'm going to just sit back and let you do it then.
<v Trastavien Hardy>他的自信，那种他用男中音说话的方式，你忍不住会觉得，好吧，是的，先生。那我还是坐下来让你来处理吧。

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:34.140
<v Jack Bailey>I'm sure that here I was, this old, rich, fat, ugly, white guy telling this young black man the way of the world and the lay of the land. And I'm sure he thought man, what have I gotten stuck with?
<v Jack Bailey>我敢肯定，当时我作为一个又老、又有钱、又胖、又丑的白人，告诉这个年轻的黑人这个世界的规矩。我敢肯定他在想，天哪，我这是被什么人缠上了？

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:52.830
<v David Zax>I've talked to a couple defense attorneys about Jack's strategy to go to trial. And they agreed it was risky. A public defender who was in the courtroom that day told me she thought it was reckless. Because first of all, if your client is interested in a plea deal, you're supposed to explore that, see what the DA might be willing to offer. Jack refused to do that.
<v David Zax>我和几个辩护律师谈过Jack要去审判的策略。他们一致认为这很冒险。那天在法庭上的一位公设辩护人告诉我，她认为这很鲁莽。因为首先，如果你的客户对认罪协议感兴趣，你应该去探讨它，看看地区检察官可能愿意提供什么。Jack拒绝这样做。

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:59.460
<v David Zax>And Trastavien was right, he really could face a lot more time if he went to trial. This was, after all, Caddo Parish.
<v David Zax>而且Trastavien是对的，如果他真的去审判，他确实可能面临更多刑期。这毕竟是卡多教区。

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:07.580
<v Derwyn Bunton>Caddo Parish is unique for how harsh their system is and can be.
<v Derwyn Bunton>卡多教区因其体制严厉而独树一帜。

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:16.400
<v David Zax>This is Derwyn Bunton, a public defender who spent the early part of his career working cases in Caddo. These days, he's the chief public defender across the state in New Orleans.
<v David Zax>这是Derwyn Bunton，一位在职业生涯早期曾在卡多处理案件的公设辩护人。如今，他是全州路易斯安那州新奥尔良的首席公设辩护人。

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:31.000
<v Derwyn Bunton>Caddo Parish has a reputation for tough, long sentences. And up until recently, led the state in capital convictions, in death sentences for a number of years.
<v Derwyn Bunton>卡多教区以严厉、长期判决而闻名。直到最近，多年来一直领先全州进行死刑判决。

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:42.120
<v David Zax>Derwyn told me the story of one case he handled in Caddo. He was representing a 14-year-old who coincidentally was charged with the same crime as Trastavien, simple burglary of an inhabited dwelling.
<v David Zax>Derwyn给我讲了他曾在卡多处理的一个案子的故事。他代表一名14岁的少年，巧合的是，他被指控的罪名和Trastavien一样，即简单入室盗窃居住房屋。

00:25:42.120 --> 00:26:06.730
<v Derwyn Bunton>And the allegation was he went into a neighbor's house without permission and took a Pepsi and a Hot Pocket. So he drank the Pepsi, ate the Hot Pocket, knew the neighbor. He got caught. And he got sentenced to seven years. He was going to stay in juvenile prison until he was 21.
<v Derwyn Bunton>指控称他未经许可进入邻居家，拿了一瓶百事可乐和一个热口袋。所以他喝了百事可乐，吃了热口袋，认识邻居。他被抓住了。他被判了七年。他得待在少年监狱直到21岁。

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:33.240
<v Derwyn Bunton>And I just thought that there was some sort of typo. No damage, no one was hurt, and he was 14. And they're going to leave him in jail till he's essentially a grown man. We were able to get him out early with some good work. But they gave him seven years without blinking and didn't even go to trial. That's Caddo Parish.
<v Derwyn Bunton>我当时只是认为那一定是打字错误。没有损坏，没人受伤，而且他才14岁。他们竟然要让他待在监狱里直到他基本上变成一个成年人。我们通过一些出色的工作让他提前出来了。但他们毫不眨眼地判了他七年，甚至都没进行审判。这就是卡多教区。

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:55.110
<v David Zax>Attorneys told me if Trastavien took a plea, he'd probably get about the same as that teenager, five to eight years. But if you push for trial in lost, the minimum he'd get, given his priors, would be 20 years. And he could get life.
<v David Zax>律师们告诉我，如果Trastavien选择认罪，他可能得到和那个青少年差不多的判决，五到八年。但如果你坚持审判而输掉，考虑到他的前科，他至少会得到20年。而且他可能被判终身监禁。

00:26:55.110 --> 00:27:11.990
<v David Zax>So through the summer, Jack and Trastavien have this series of pre-trial court dates. The main reason they're noteworthy is because at every one, Jack is being a giant pain in the ass, demanding a speedy trial, berating the young DA on this case, insisting they free Trastavien without bail.
<v David Zax>所以整个夏天，Jack和Trastavien进行了一系列审前庭日期。他们值得注意的主要原因是，每一次Jack都在制造麻烦，要求快速审判，斥责处理此案的年轻地区检察官，坚持在没有保释金的情况下释放Trastavien。

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:32.980
<v David Zax>Trastavien says people in the stands were oohing and ahhing at Jack's performance. One old woman said, God is good. A trial date was set for November 30. And the judge released Trastavien to wait out the days till trial at home. At that point, he'd spent 105 days in jail.
<v David Zax>Trastavien说旁听席上的人对Jack的表现发出惊叹。一位老妇人说，上帝是仁慈的。审判日期定在11月30日。法官释放了Trastavien，让他回家等待审判。那时，他在监狱里待了105天。

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:51.530
<v David Zax>Here's the riskiest thing about Jack's demand for a speedy trial, he demanded it without even knowing all the evidence the state had on Trastavien. Not till September did that big box of files Tammy was expecting finally come in from the DA's office. It was more of a packet, 54 pages stuffed into a manila envelope.
<v David Zax>这是Jack要求快速审判最冒险的一点，他甚至不知道政府对Trastavien掌握的所有证据就提出了要求。直到9月，Tammy一直期待的那大箱档案才终于从地方检察官办公室寄来。那更像是一个数据包，塞进马尼拉纸信封的54页纸。

00:27:51.530 --> 00:28:18.320
<v David Zax>Tammy tore into it as soon as it came. And it turned out that the case against Trastavien was even weaker than they thought. Because the crime lab report, the one that pointed out Trastavien's DNA had been found on a hat, it said there was actually DNA from two people on the hat. Someone else besides Trastavien had worn that hat. So they had a good case after all.
<v David Zax>Tammy一拿到就打开了。结果针对Trastavien的案子比他们想象的还要薄弱。因为犯罪实验室报告，也就是指出Trastavien的DNA在帽子上被发现的那份，上面说帽子上实际上有两个人的DNA。除了Trastavien之外还有别人戴过那顶帽子。所以他们毕竟有一个很好的案子。

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:27.920
<v David Zax>It's the Saturday after Thanksgiving, two days before the trial that could send Trastavien away for decades. Jack and Tammy are supposed to be preparing, but when Tammy gets to the office--
<v David Zax>那是感恩节后的星期六，距离可能让Trastavien入狱几十年的审判还有两天。Jack和Tammy应该正在准备，但当Tammy来到办公室时——

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:32.960
<v Tammy Burdine>I have a note in my chair that says I'm sick.
<v Tammy Burdine>我的椅子上有一张便条，写着我病了。

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:37.130
<v David Zax>The note, of course, was from Jack. He came into work, just wanted to complain.
<v David Zax>这张便条当然是Jack写的。他来到工作岗位，只是想抱怨。

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:41.790
<v Jack Bailey>I got some horrible Siberian death crud upper respiratory infection.
<v Jack Bailey>我得了可怕的西伯利亚死亡病毒，上呼吸道感染。

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:47.870
<v Tammy Burdine>And I'm like what?
<v Tammy Burdine>我心想什么？

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:57.720
<v Jack Bailey>Instead of being at home Thanksgiving with my family, Tammy and I are up here getting prepared for trial. We're working on PowerPoints, timelines.
<v Jack Bailey>感恩节没有和家人待在家里，Tammy和我却在这里为审判做准备。我们正在制作PowerPoint，时间表。

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:08.360
<v Tammy Burdine>Preparing documents, making sure I's are dotted and T's are crossed. Because I'm freaking out. I am freaking out.
<v Tammy Burdine>准备文件，确保每一处细节都无懈可击。因为我快疯了。我快疯了。

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:17.240
<v Jack Bailey>Years ago I had gotten a series of videotapes, VHS tapes from Gerry Spence about voir dire and opening statement.
<v Jack Bailey>几年前，我得到了一系列录像带，Gerry Spence关于陪审团挑选和开场陈词的VHS录像带。

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:22.080
<v David Zax>Gerry Spence is a famed trial lawyer, a kind of legal guru. Voire dire is jury selection.
<v David Zax>Gerry Spence是一位著名的庭审律师，某种法律大师。Voire dire就是陪审团挑选。

00:29:22.080 --> 00:29:44.650
<v Jack Bailey>And I pulled my old TV out in the back that you can still play VHS tapes in. And I'm sitting in here, you know, drinking tea and eating ibuprofen and watching Gerry Spence tapes, taking notes to remember how to do this the right way, you know?
<v Jack Bailey>我把后面那台还能播放VHS录像带的旧电视机拉了出来。我坐在那儿，你知道，喝着茶，吃着布洛芬，看着Gerry Spence的录像带，做笔记，记着如何以正确的方式来做这件事，你知道吗？

00:29:44.650 --> 00:30:05.560
<v David Zax>Monday, November 30, the day of the trial. Jack, Tammy, and Trastavien all enter the court house. Jack's eager. He's ready. He loves this part of the job. But before anything official begins, the young DA on this case, Scott Brady, he pulls Jack aside and tells him something totally shocking. He's going to nolle pros the case.
<v David Zax>11月30日星期一，审判日。Jack、Tammy和Trastavien都进入了法院。Jack很急切。他准备好了。他热爱工作的这一部分。但在任何正式程序开始之前，此案的年轻地区检察官Scott Brady把Jack拉到一边，告诉了他一件非常令人震惊的事。他要撤诉。

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:14.880
<v Jack Bailey>In other words, I'm dropping the charges. And at that point I said some things that I can't tell you if you're going to play them on the radio.
<v Jack Bailey>换句话说，我要撤销指控。在那一点上，我说了一些如果要在广播中播放我就不能告诉你的话。

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.070
<v Producer>Why don't  you tell us and then will determine if we--
<v Producer>为什么你不告诉我们，然后我们再决定是否——

