<v Jack Bailey>I refused to leave the courtroom without putting some things on the record.
<v Jack Bailey>我拒绝离开法庭，而不把一些事情记入记录。

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:30.740
<v David Zax>Several defense attorneys told me this was a pretty crazy thing Jack was doing. The DA was ready to dismiss the charges. Normally, you'd say thank you and move on. What if Jack said something that made the DA change his mind? Tammy was worried.
<v David Zax>几位辩护律师告诉我，Jack正在做的这件事相当疯狂。地区检察官已经准备好撤销指控。通常，你会说谢谢然后继续。如果Jack说了什么让地区检察官改变主意怎么办？Tammy很担心。

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:49.550
<v Tammy Burdine>And now I'm going to pass out right here in this chair. What is he doing? What is happening? And Mr Hardy had been standing up there with him. And so he's really, he turns around and looks at me like is this OK?
<v Tammy Burdine>而现在我就要晕倒在这把椅子上了。他在做什么？发生了什么？Hardy先生一直站在他旁边。所以他真的，他转过身看着我，好像在问这样可以吗？

00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:53.000
<v Trastavien Hardy>Really just looked back like whoa, what's going on now?
<v Trastavien Hardy>确实只是回头看了看，好像在问，哇，现在发生什么了？

00:32:53.000 --> 00:33:13.110
<v Tammy Burdine>And I'm sure my look told him, this is not OK on any level. This is not OK. You should panic. Come sit down. Get away from him. Stop. Because I do know Mr. Bailey. And he's going to say what he believes needs to be said.
<v Tammy Burdine>我敢肯定我的表情告诉他，这在任何层面上都不可以。这不可以。你应该恐慌。快坐下。离他远点。停下。因为我确实了解Bailey先生。他会说出他认为需要说出的话。

00:33:13.110 --> 00:33:32.800
<v Jack Bailey>I said judge, I have a statement I want to make for the record to protect Mr Hardy in the future if this arrest prosecution is ever held against him if you don't mind. I said judge, this man is factually innocent. And I'm sorry the court is not going to get to see me excoriate the police and the district attorney's office in this case.
<v Jack Bailey>我说法官，我有一个声明要记入记录，以便在未来保护Hardy先生，如果这次逮捕起诉被视为对他不利，如果你不介意的话。我说法官，这个男人在事实上是无罪的。我很抱歉法庭将无法看到我在这个案件中严厉抨击警方和地区检察官办公室。

00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:40.850
<v David Zax>Jack's speech lasted about 10 minutes. He ranted about the scant evidence against his client, about the DA, about having to take this case at all.
<v David Zax>Jack的演讲持续了大约10分钟。他咆哮着针对他客户的薄弱证据，针对地区检察官，针对不得不接这个案子本身。

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:45.740
<v Jack Bailey>So you can put on the record, judge, I'm angry, and I'm angry at the system.
<v Jack Bailey>所以你可以记入记录，法官，我很愤怒，我对这个体制感到愤怒。

00:33:45.740 --> 00:34:10.030
<v David Zax>When Jack was done, the judge then turned to Scott Brady and asked, Mr. DA, anything further? The DA, Scott Brady, answered the judge, no, sir. The matter has been dismissed. The judge turned to Trastavien, you're free to go, he said.
<v David Zax>当Jack说完后，法官转向Scott Brady问道，地方检察官先生，还有什么吗？地区检察官Scott Brady回答法官，没有了，先生。事情已经被撤销了。法官转向Trastavien，你自由了，他说。

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:25.960
<v David Zax>Since Scott Brady won't do an interview, we don't know why he dropped the case. There are a few factors here. The victim didn't show up in court. And the crime, in the scheme of things, wasn't that serious. But both public defenders who were in the courtroom that day told me they thought Jack might have been the deciding factor.
<v David Zax>由于Scott Brady不做采访，我们不知道他为什么要撤销案子。这里有几个因素。受害者那天没出庭。而且在事情的发展中，犯罪并没有那么严重。但那天在法庭上的两位公设辩护人都告诉我，他们认为Jack可能是决定性因素。

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:32.340
<v David Zax>One of them, Sarah Smith, said Jack made it clear he was going to fight tooth and nail on every procedural matter.
<v David Zax>其中一位Sarah Smith说，Jack清楚地表明他会在每一个程序问题上针锋相对。

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:46.190
<v Sarah Smith>The motions that Jack was making, the 10 minute speeches, and not necessarily following the traditional order of this is how criminal court is supposed to work. Jack definitely wasn't making this an easy process.
<v Sarah Smith>Jack提出的动议，那10分钟的演讲，并不一定遵循传统的顺序，这是刑事法庭应该运作的方式。Jack绝对没有让这个过程变得容易。

00:34:46.190 --> 00:35:02.650
<v David Zax>She says a normal public defender doesn't have time to fight like that for all their clients. And once Jack made things so difficult, the DA might have decided he didn't have time to deal with it either.
<v David Zax>她说，一个普通的公设辩护人没时间为他们所有的客户那样奋斗。而一旦Jack把事情变得如此困难，地方检察官可能决定他也没时间去处理了。

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:16.020
<v David Zax>Trastavien's been free for over a year now. He got a job as a fry cook at a Sonic Drive-In. He says he wants to start school again next year. And he's got a little girl, Carla. He's spending a lot of time with her.
<v David Zax>Trastavien已经自由一年多了。他在Sonic Drive-In找到了一份煎炸厨师的工作。他说他想明年重新开始上学。他有一个小女儿，Carla。他花了很多时间陪她。

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:40.700
<v David Zax>Right as this story was taking place, Caddo Parish elected a new top DA, the first black DA in its history. One of his first actions was getting rid of nine assistant district attorneys in his office. Scott Brady, who prosecuted the case against Trastavien, was among them. Just this month, the public defender's office in Caddo Parish stopped the practice of farming out cases to private attorneys. Some funding came through, for now.
<v David Zax>就在这个故事发生的同时，卡多教区选举了一位新的顶级地区检察官，这是该教区历史上第一位黑人地方检察官。他的第一个行动之一就是解雇办公室里的九名助理地方检察官。处理针对Trastavien案件的Scott Brady就在其中。就在本月，卡多教区的公设辩护人办公室停止了将案件分包给私人律师的做法。一些资金通过了，目前为止。

00:35:40.700 --> 00:36:05.900
<v David Zax>Jack, meanwhile, has been pushing for more funding for public defenders. He went to the Louisiana State Legislature, met with three state senators and the public defender lobbyist. He offered to testify. He said he'd do anything to help. He does not want another case like this.
<v David Zax>与此同时，Jack一直在推动为公设辩护人提供更多资助。他去了路易斯安那州议会，会见了三名州参议员和公设辩护人的游说者。他主动提出作证。他说他会做任何事来帮忙。他不希望再有这样的案子。

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:20.550
<v Ira Glass>David Zax. Coming up, how a public park in the suburbs of Virginia helped beat the Nazis. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>David Zax。接下来，弗吉尼亚州郊区的一个公共公园是如何帮助打败纳粹的。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:39.200
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, Deep End of the Pool. Stories of people thrown into serious situations they have no skills for, where they have to figure out what to do on the fly. We have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Mein Camp.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》，我是Ira Glass。当然，每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来关于该主题的不同种类的故事。今天的节目，泳池深水区。关于人们被扔进他们没有技能的严重处境中的故事，他们必须在飞行中弄清楚该做什么。我们已经到达了我们节目的第二幕。第二幕，我的奋斗。

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:57.420
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes an entire government has to dive into the deep end of the pool and do something it has never done before. One of our producers, Karen Duffin, has the story of the US government doing just that in a high stakes sink or swim situation. Though the reason we know about this government program at all is actually kind of an accident. Karen explains.
<v Ira Glass>有时整个政府必须潜入深水区，做一些它从未做过的事。我们的制作人之一Karen Duffin，有关于美国政府在生死攸关的“沉还是游”情况下做这件事的故事。虽然我们了解这个政府计划的原因其实有点意外。Karen解释道。

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:15.530
<v Karen Duffin>Back in 2006, a woman named Sandy Gray decided to go on a tour of the park near her house in suburban Virginia. It's a national park, but less like Yellowstone, more like a neighborhood park-- soccer fields, softball diamonds, picnic pavilions. Over 100 acres. It's called Fort Hunt Park.
<v Karen Duffin>早在2006年，一位叫Sandy Gray的女士决定去弗吉尼亚州郊区她家附近的公园游览。这是一个国家公园，但不像黄石公园，更像是一个邻里公园——足球场、垒球场、野餐亭。超过100英亩。它叫Fort Hunt公园。

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:29.710
<v Karen Duffin>The park ranger ran through the history of the place. She told Sandy's group it had been part of George Washington's estate. She pointed to structures from the Spanish-American war. But when she got to World War II, she said that is a mystery.
<v Karen Duffin>公园管理员介绍了这个地方的历史。她告诉Sandy的小组，这曾经是乔治·华盛顿庄园的一部分。她指出了西班牙-美国战争时期的建筑。但当她谈到二战时，她说那是一个谜。

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:45.160
<v Karen Duffin>We know there was a secret military program here, code named PO Box 1142. She said the rangers had been trying for years to figure out what it was, even asked the Pentagon about it. But they said they thought the files had been destroyed.
<v Karen Duffin>我们知道这里有一个秘密军事计划，代号PO Box 1142。她说管理员们多年来一直试图弄清楚它是什么，甚至问过五角大楼。但他们说他们认为文件已经被销毁了。

00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:51.180
<v Sandy Gray>And when we got to this point, she said we probably never will know the whole story.
<v Sandy Gray>当我们到达这一点时，她说我们可能永远不会知道全部真相。

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:55.780
<v Karen Duffin>This is Sandy. Turned out she knew something the rangers didn't.
<v Karen Duffin>这是Sandy。结果她知道一些管理员们不知道的事。

00:37:55.780 --> 00:38:12.570
<v Sandy Gray>So I was thinking whether or not I should say anything because I was questioning whether I was supposed to know anything. So I raised my hand and I said you know, I think I can hook you up with someone who was here during that time.
<v Sandy Gray>所以我当时在想我是否应该说出来，因为我质疑我是否应该知道任何事情。所以我举手说你知道，我想我可以帮你联系一个在那期间在这里待过的人。

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:29.940
<v Karen Duffin>Sandy had never heard of PO Box 1142. But she remembered that her neighbor had said he'd worked at the site during the war. So she gave the rangers his name, Fred Michel. And later, back at the office, they huddled around a speaker phone and they dialed his number.
<v Karen Duffin>Sandy从未听说过PO Box 1142。但她记得她的邻居说过他在战争期间在那里工作过。所以她给了管理员们他的名字，Fred Michel。后来，回到办公室，他们挤在扬声器电话旁拨了他的号码。

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:37.720
<v Karen Duffin>The head park ranger at the time was Vince Santucci. He told Fred they were calling about PO Box 1142.
<v Karen Duffin>当时的首席公园管理员是Vince Santucci。他告诉Fred他们打电话是关于PO Box 1142的。

00:38:37.720 --> 00:39:02.300
<v Vince Santucci>And there was a little bit of silence. And the first comment that comes back is how do you know about PO Box 1142? So we went in to explain what we were doing. And his response was that he's not able to share anything with us because he signed a secrecy agreement. In fact, that he didn't tell his wife of 65 years. And he said, I wish I could share it with you, but he couldn't.
<v Vince Santucci>有一点沉默。第一个回来的评价是你是怎么知道PO Box 1142的？所以我们解释了我们在做什么。他的回答是他不能和我们分享任何事情，因为他签署了保密协议。事实上，他甚至没告诉他结婚65年的妻子。他说，我希望我能和你分享，但他做不到。

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:17.750
<v Karen Duffin>The rangers hung up and panicked. They worried Fred might be their only shot. World War II vets were in their 80s, 90s, or dead. So they went back to the Pentagon. And said, we found a guy, but he won't talk. Can you help?
<v Karen Duffin>管理员们挂断电话并惊慌失措。他们担心Fred可能是他们唯一的希望。二战老兵当时已经80多岁、90多岁，或者去世了。所以他们回到五角大楼。说，我们找到了一个人，但他不肯说。你能帮忙吗？

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:22.940
<v Karen Duffin>So the director of Army Intelligence wrote Fred a letter clearing him to speak. Here's Vince.
<v Karen Duffin>所以陆军情报局局长给Fred写了一封信，清除他讲话的障碍。这是Vince。

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:28.040
<v Vince Santucci>And so when we forwarded that to Fred Michel, he said, OK, what do you want to know?
<v Vince Santucci>所以当我们把它转发给Fred Michel时，他说，好吧，你想知道什么？

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:30.460
<v Karen Duffin>The rangers went to Fred's house to interview him.
<v Karen Duffin>管理员们去了Fred家采访他。

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:38.260
<v Brandon Bies>I'm here in the home of Frederick and Lucille Michel conducting a series of oral history interviews about their memories of Fort Hunt.
<v Brandon Bies>我现在在Frederick和Lucille Michel的家中，进行一系列关于他们对Fort Hunt记忆的口述历史采访。

00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:48.970
<v Karen Duffin>This is park ranger Brandon Bies. He did a lot of these interviews. And for 4 and 1/2 hours, Fred told them about PO Box 1142. Then he gave them more names.
<v Karen Duffin>这是公园管理员Brandon Bies。他做了很多这样的采访。在4个半小时里，Fred告诉他们关于PO Box 1142的事情。然后他给了他们更多的名字。

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:51.900
<v Fred Michel>I was transferred to PO Box 1142.
<v Fred Michel>我被转到了PO Box 1142。

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:53.860
<v Karen Duffin>They made more calls, did more interviews.
<v Karen Duffin>他们打了更多的电话，做了更多的采访。

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:56.890
<v Werner Moritz>We were told that we're not going to talk, not to our wives, not to anybody.
<v Werner Moritz>我们被告知我们要保持沉默，不能对我们的妻子，不能对任何人。

00:39:56.890 --> 00:39:59.510
<v Henry Kolm>We got the photographs from the Pentagon. And everything--
<v Henry Kolm>我们从五角大楼得到了照片。还有一切——

00:39:59.510 --> 00:40:23.340
<v Karen Duffin>They talked to 70 men. For over 60 years, these men had all been living with the same secret. This suburban park had been a top secret camp for interrogating Nazi prisoners of war. And the men doing the interrogations, they were Jews. Lots of them, anyway. European Jews who just fled the Nazis. Many still had family in concentration camps.
<v Karen Duffin>他们和70个人谈过。60多年来，这些人一直活在同一个秘密中。这个郊区公园曾经是一个审问纳粹战俘的绝密营地。而进行审问的人，他们是犹太人。很多人，至少是很多。逃离纳粹的欧洲犹太人。许多人仍有家人在集中营里。

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:52.810
<v Karen Duffin>These men were specifically recruited because they spoke German, and because they understood the nuances of German culture and psychology, slang, cultural references, small details that an American would miss. This was the American military's first attempt to create a program like this for interrogating prisoners. First time we approached interrogation in a systematic and strategic way, not just to learn things like where's the bomb about to go off, but to get a whole picture of the enemy's plans.
<v Karen Duffin>这些人被专门招募是因为他们会说德语，并且因为他们理解德国文化和心理的细微差别、俚语、文化参考、美国人会错过的微小细节。这是美国军方第一次尝试创建这样一个审问程序。我们第一次以系统和战略的方式接近审问，不仅仅是为了了解炸弹在哪里要爆炸，而是为了得到敌人计划的整体图景。

00:40:52.810 --> 00:41:24.660
<v Karen Duffin>And they were figuring out how to do this in the middle of the deadliest armed conflict in world history. And they succeeded. By the end of the war, they'd extracted a ton of really important intelligence about where the Allies should bomb, about German weapons still being developed, about the structure of the German Army. One of the Enigma machines was captured using intel discovered at PO Box 1142.
<v Karen Duffin>他们当时正在想如何在世界历史上最致命的武装冲突中弄清楚如何做这件事。而且他们成功了。战争结束时，他们提取了大量关于盟军应该在哪里轰炸、关于仍在开发的德国武器、关于德国军队结构的非常重要的情报。其中一台Enigma机器是在利用在PO Box 1142发现的情报捕获的。

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:39.880
<v Karen Duffin>The approach they used didn't include any kind of physical force. In the hundreds of previously classified files that I've read, torture is never considered. Instead, they used this approach. This is from a World War II interrogation training film.
<v Karen Duffin>他们使用的方法不包括任何形式的身体力量。在我读过的数百份之前被列为机密的文件中，从未考虑过酷刑。相反，他们使用了这种方法。这是来自二战审问培训电影。

00:41:39.880 --> 00:41:49.130
<v Trainer>Make your interrogation as casual as possible. Try to make the prisoner feel that you're his friend, the first one he's met since his capture.
<v Trainer>使你的审问尽可能随意。尽量让囚犯感觉到你是他的朋友，他是被捕以来遇到的第一个朋友。

00:41:49.130 --> 00:41:58.100
<v Trainer 2>All are human underneath. Our interrogator's job is to play upon those weaknesses, to help make up the complete intelligence picture.
<v Trainer 2>所有人在内心深处都是人类。我们审问者的工作就是利用那些弱点，帮助构建完整的情报图景。

00:41:58.100 --> 00:42:01.130
<v Karen Duffin>And here's a mock interrogation from the film.
<v Karen Duffin>这是电影中的模拟审问。

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:03.560
<v Interegator>Why did you have this Spanish dictionary in your plane?
<v Interegator>你为什么要在飞机上放这本西班牙语字典？

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:04.780
<v Prisoner>Just improving my mind.
<v Prisoner>只是为了改善我的思想。

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:11.160
<v Interegator>Who do you think you're fooling, Tenetti? Your name is right there on the cover. Why were you flying back to Spain? You must not lie to me.
<v Interegator>你以为你在愚弄谁，Tenetti？你的名字就在封面上。你为什么飞回西班牙？你不能对我撒谎。

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:12.260
<v Prisoner>I only tell the truth.
<v Prisoner>我只说实话。

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:14.120
<v Interegator>Now don't try to give me that routine.
<v Interegator>现在不要试着给我那套程序。

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:21.710
<v Trainer 2>Time. Time, gentlemen. We'll stop there. I'm going to ask for criticisms. Any volunteers? Yes, lieutenant?
<v Trainer 2>时间。时间，先生们。我们就停在那儿。我要请求批评。有志愿者吗？是的，中尉？

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:27.920
<v Lieutenant>It's not good to lose your temper that way, especially with such a dumb, good-natured type.
<v Lieutenant>那样发脾气不好，尤其是对这种愚蠢、随和的类型。

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:40.560
<v Karen Duffin>It seems self-evident now, but at the time, it was sort of a revelation that you could get information by playing on a prisoner's psychology. At the end of another mock interrogation, the instructor reviewed what worked on that prisoner.
<v Karen Duffin>现在看来是不言自明的，但在当时，通过利用囚犯的心理来获取信息是一种启示。在另一次模拟审问结束时，导师回顾了对那个囚犯什么管用。

00:42:40.560 --> 00:43:06.520
<v Trainer 2>You noticed how he won the prisoner's gratitude by promising to inform his family of his safety. How he established immediate contact through mutual interest in the Munich theater. How he played on the Bavarian's prejudice against his Prussian lieutenant. How he pretended to know more about the new Heinkel 177s than the Sergeant, thereby piquing his vanity and causing him to talk. As a result, he is now in possession of valuable information.
<v Trainer 2>你注意到他是如何通过承诺通知他的家人他的安全来赢得囚犯的感激了吗？他是如何通过对慕尼黑剧院的共同兴趣建立即时联系的？他是如何利用巴伐利亚人对他的普鲁士中尉的偏见来打牌的？他是如何假装比中士更了解新的Heinkel 177，从而激起他的虚荣心并导致他说话的。结果，他现在拥有了有价值的信息。

00:43:06.520 --> 00:43:18.660
<v Karen Duffin>It's like a high stakes game of psychological poker. To win, you have to see the enemy in front of you as a nuanced human. They were taught that even feeling too much hostility about the enemy would blind them.
<v Karen Duffin>这就像是一场高风险的心理扑克游戏。为了赢，你必须把你面前的敌人视为一个细微的人。他们被教导说，即使对敌人感觉太多的敌意也会使他们盲目。

00:43:18.660 --> 00:43:33.930
<v Karen Duffin>I spent years studying this camp. And what I wanted to know was the most basic thing. What it was like for these Jewish men facing off with the Nazis in such a personal way. Facing people who'd driven some of them out of their country, killed their families.
<v Karen Duffin>我花了几年时间研究这个营地。而我想知道的是最基本的事情。对于这些犹太人来说，以这样一种私人的方式面对纳粹是什么感觉。面对那些把他们中的一些人赶出国、杀害了他们家人的人。

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:50.660
<v Karen Duffin>So I pestered the government until they released over 1,000 pages of interviews with these guys. And I personally spoke with 10 of them or their families if they weren't still alive. And in this mountain of material, many of them said they were happy to do the work. It meant helping beat the Nazis.
<v Karen Duffin>所以我一直纠缠政府，直到他们发布了1000多页对这些家伙的采访。而且我亲自和其中10个人或他们的家人谈过，如果他们还没去世的话。在这堆山一样的材料中，许多人说他们很高兴做这份工作。这意味着帮助打败纳粹。

00:43:50.660 --> 00:44:06.240
<v Karen Duffin>But some had more complicated feelings, like this guy, Paul Fairbrook, who the park rangers interviewed in 2008. He was German, Jewish. Said he agreed with not using force on the Nazis, but befriending them, no way.
<v Karen Duffin>但有些人有更复杂的感情，比如这个家伙，Paul Fairbrook，公园管理员在2008年采访了他。他是德国人，犹太人。说他同意不对纳粹使用武力，但与他们交朋友，没门。

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:27.110
<v Paul Fairbrook>Many of us German Jewish soldiers resented the fact that some of our offices were fraternizing with German officers, Nazis. Because every German officer was a Nazi, you know. And here we are fighting as German Jews for America and these jerks, our officers, are fraternizing with the people who were our enemies.
<v Paul Fairbrook>我们中的许多德国犹太士兵对我们的一些办公室在与德国军官、纳粹分子亲善感到不满。因为每个德国军官都是纳粹，你知道。而我们在这里作为德国犹太人为美国而战，而这些混蛋，我们的军官，却在与我们的敌人亲善。

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:34.520
<v Karen Duffin>But then there was this guy, Henry Kolm. He says that working that closely with the Nazis changed his opinion of them.
<v Karen Duffin>但后来有这个家伙，Henry Kolm。他说与纳粹如此密切地工作改变了他对他们的看法。

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:46.690
<v Henry Kolm>When you get to know somebody one-on-one you know, it's a different story. It proved to me that there are decent people in Germany, despite the Third Reich and Nazi and Hitler and all the rest of it.
<v Henry Kolm>当你一对一了解某人时，你知道，这是另一回事。它证明了尽管有第三帝国和纳粹和希特勒以及所有这一切，德国仍有体面的人。

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:56.630
<v Karen Duffin>He genuinely liked some of them. But still, when he was assigned to guard duty, he took extra precautions, just in case the prisoners realized he was Jewish.
<v Karen Duffin>他真心喜欢其中一些人。但仍然，当他被指派到守卫职责时，他采取了额外的预防措施，以防万一囚犯意识到他是犹太人。

00:44:56.630 --> 00:45:05.080
<v Henry Kolm>And I often spent all day inside that stockade. And that was not a very comfortable feeling. I had a pistol in my sock.
<v Henry Kolm>我经常整天待在那个围栏里面。那感觉真不舒服。我在袜子里放了一把手枪。

00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:09.700
<v Karen Duffin>He said he had a pistol in his sock. They didn't normally carry guns.
<v Karen Duffin>他说他在袜子里放了一把手枪。他们通常不携带枪支。

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:20.720
<v Henry Kolm>Yeah, I didn't feel very happy in a courtyard surrounded by 60 German soldiers, prisoners. You know, I'm Jewish, and they might have known that.
<v Henry Kolm>是的，我在一个被60名德国士兵、囚犯包围的庭院里感觉不太好。你知道，我是犹太人，他们可能知道这一点。

00:45:20.720 --> 00:45:30.960
<v Karen Duffin>But in the 80 or so hours of interviews, I found only one incident where someone who was trained at 1142 just flipped out on a German prisoner.
<v Karen Duffin>但在80多小时的采访中，我只发现了一个事件，在1142受过训练的人对德国囚犯失去了理智。

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:33.230
<v Werner Moritz>I'm going to talk as it comes out of my mouth.
<v Werner Moritz>我要说出它从我嘴里出来的样子。

00:45:33.230 --> 00:45:34.980
<v Brandon Bies>That's that point.
<v Brandon Bies>这是那个点。

00:45:34.980 --> 00:45:59.950
<v Karen Duffin>This is Werner Moritz. He died in 2010, so I never met him, but I talked with his daughter. She said he was a man of strong opinions, the kind of man who walks into a room and tells a joke just so that you he's there. Sharp dresser, mustache always neatly trimmed. He spent two years at PO Box 1142, where he says he was trained like everyone else not to mistreat prisoners. And he never did at the camp.
<v Karen Duffin>这是Werner Moritz。他于2010年去世，所以我从未见过他，但我与他的女儿谈过。她说他是一个有强烈观点的人，那种走进房间讲个笑话只是为了让你知道他在那儿的男人。衣着光鲜，胡子总是修剪得很整齐。他在PO Box 1142待了两年，他说他像其他人一样被训练不要虐待囚犯。他在营地里从未这样做过。

00:45:59.950 --> 00:46:09.520
<v Karen Duffin>But near the end of the war, Werner was sent to Europe to help find and interrogate Nazis who now understood they were losing and were hiding out.
<v Karen Duffin>但在战争快结束时，Werner被派往欧洲，帮助寻找和审问现在明白他们正在失败并躲藏起来的纳粹。

00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:20.050
<v Werner Moritz>We were given orders at that point, he said, to just roam around. Find the Nazis. Find the bigwigs, find the big shots. So we started to roam.
<v Werner Moritz>当时我们接到命令，他说，四处转转。找到纳粹。找到大人物，找到大腕。所以我们开始四处转转。

00:46:20.050 --> 00:46:42.230
<v Karen Duffin>And one day, he says he found a high ranking Nazi named Julius Streicher. Streicher was a friend of Hitler. He had masterminded some of the nastiest anti-semitic propaganda. His newspaper, Der Stürmer, played a huge role in fueling the hatred towards the Jews that led to the Holocaust. The paper's tag line was "The Jews are our misfortune."
<v Karen Duffin>有一天，他说他找到了一个叫Julius Streicher的高级纳粹。Streicher是希特勒的朋友。他策划了一些最恶心的反犹太宣传。他的报纸《Der Stürmer》在助长导致大屠杀的对犹太人的仇恨方面发挥了巨大作用。报纸的标签是“犹太人是我们的不幸”。

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:48.950
<v Karen Duffin>So he was a man that Germans, especially German Jews, were familiar with. So when Werner found him--
<v Karen Duffin>所以他是一个德国人，尤其是德国犹太人熟悉的人。所以当Werner找到他时——

00:46:48.950 --> 00:47:06.970
<v Werner Moritz>I was enraged. I was trembling. There were tears in my eyes that I had captured this guy. I had him to myself. Then I had him handcuffed, put him in the Jeep. And then we come to this little hut. Then we put him in there.
<v Werner Moritz>我被激怒了。我一直在颤抖。我眼里含着泪水，因为我抓住了这个家伙。我独自拥有了他。然后我给他戴上手铐，把他放进吉普车。然后我们来到这个小屋。然后我们把他放进去。

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:21.900
<v Karen Duffin>Werner's mother and sister were killed in concentration camps. He'd spent three months in Buchenwald himself in 1938. So face to face with Streicher, he made a decision. A warning to listeners, the next part gets a little graphic.
<v Karen Duffin>Werner的母亲和姐妹被杀害在集中营里。他自己1938年在布痕瓦尔德待了三个月。所以面对Streicher，他做了一个决定。提醒听众，下一部分有点血腥。

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:49.430
<v Werner Moritz>I had to explain to the MPs. I'm going to do things, you probably think I'm crazy. And you want to know something? I am crazy. I'm crazed. I captured a Nazi of unbelievable mischief. I captured a Nazi who doesn't deserve to live. I would like to shoot him right now, but I know I would be court marshaled. But I gonna, I'm gonna do what I have to do.
<v Werner Moritz>我不得不向军事警察解释。我要做一些事，你们可能认为我疯了。你想知道一些事吗？我是疯了。我疯了。我抓了一个不可思议的纳粹恶棍。我抓了一个不配活下去的纳粹。我真想现在就枪毙他，但我知道我会被送上军事法庭。但我会，我会做我必须做的事。

00:47:49.430 --> 00:48:02.430
<v Werner Moritz>I had him undress, and his clothes throwed to the side, underwear, everything. I told him, from now on, you sleep naked on this cold floor. You will not move. You will not make a move.
<v Werner Moritz>我让他脱掉衣服，把衣服扔到一边，内衣，一切。我告诉他，从现在起，你赤身裸体睡在这个冰冷的地板上。你不能动。你不能动。

00:48:02.430 --> 00:48:16.590
<v Werner Moritz>After that, I pissed all over him. Terrible thing to tell  you. His head and everywhere. And he started to move his head and I said to him, don't move. I have my pistol in my hand, I'll shoot you in  the ass. That's how enraged I was.
<v Werner Moritz>在那之后，我在他身上撒了尿。告诉你这是一件可怕的事。他的头和到处都是。他开始移动他的头，我对他说，别动。我手里拿着手枪，我会打爆你的屁股。我当时就是这么愤怒。

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:37.490
<v Werner Moritz>I said, you're just to lie there to get some sense of what you Nazis did to the Jews. This is only a small sense. You will not be burned here. We won't cook you  in an oven. But this German Jew will heap upon you. And don't you move, or else you're a dead man.
<v Werner Moritz>我说，你就躺在那里感受一下你们纳粹对犹太人做了什么。这只是一点点感受。你不会在这里被烧。我们不会把你放在烤箱里煮。但这名德国犹太人会堆积在你身上。别动，否则你就是个死人。

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:56.820
<v Werner Moritz>And I left into my quarters and went to sleep. It's a terrible thing to tell you, but it is the truth. That's what I did. This was an event that gets me very upset, even now.
<v Werner Moritz>然后我离开了我的宿舍去睡觉。告诉你这是一件可怕的事，但这是事实。这就是我做的。这是一个让我非常不安的事件，即使现在也是如此。

00:48:56.820 --> 00:49:14.600
<v Karen Duffin>For three more days he held Streicher, refused to feed him anything but potato peels that he pissed on too. On the fourth day, Werner handed him off to American officers. Streicher was one of only 11 men sentenced to death at the Nuremberg trials.
<v Karen Duffin>他把Streicher关了三天，拒绝给他任何食物，除了他也撒了尿的土豆皮。第四天，Werner把他交给了美国军官。Streicher是纽伦堡审判中仅有的11名被判处死刑的人之一。

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:38.440
<v Karen Duffin>The specific records about Streicher's capture are spotty and contradictory. Usually someone else is credited with capturing him. But we know Werner was in the area doing this kind of work at the time. And an expert at the National Archives told me he does think Werner could have done this.
<v Karen Duffin>关于Streicher捕获的具体记录是零碎且矛盾的。通常其他人被认为抓获了他。但我们知道Werner当时正在该地区做这类工作。国家档案馆的一位专家告诉我，他确实认为Werner可能做到了这一点。

00:49:38.440 --> 00:49:47.290
<v Karen Duffin>The man who helped me understand best what it was like to do this work was this last man, also Jewish. He escaped Luxembourg and came here.
<v Karen Duffin>帮助我最好地理解做这份工作是什么感觉的男人是最后这个人，也是犹太人。他逃离了卢森堡来到这里。

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:56.480
<v Arno Mayer>Well my name is Arno Mayer. And I don't want you to be generous, because it's A-R-N-O, and most people tend to add an L-D to it, which I refuse.
<v Arno Mayer>好吧，我的名字是Arno Mayer。我不希望你大方，因为它是A-R-N-O，大多数人倾向于在它后面加一个L-D，我拒绝了。

00:49:56.480 --> 00:50:07.780
<v Karen Duffin>I met Arno at his apartment in Princeton, New Jersey. He was 88. Small guy, with a full head of white hair, brown eyebrows. He peppered our interview with historical facts and profanity.
<v Karen Duffin>我在新泽西州普林斯顿的公寓里见到了Arno。他88岁。小个子，满头白发，棕色眉毛。他在采访中夹杂着历史事实和脏话。

00:50:07.780 --> 00:50:12.690
<v Arno Mayer>They invented a name for me. The name was the Intellectual Fuck.
<v Arno Mayer>他们为我发明了一个名字。名字叫“知识分子混蛋”。

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:24.970
<v Karen Duffin>Arno was assigned to PO Box 1142 at the end of the war. By then, the mission had changed. Instead of interrogating Nazis, they were now asked to wine, dine, and woo them.
<v Karen Duffin>Arno在战争结束时被分配到PO Box 1142。到那时，任务已经改变了。不是审问纳粹，他们现在被要求用酒、晚餐和求爱来对付他们。

00:50:24.970 --> 00:50:35.260
<v Karen Duffin>Many Nazis now at the camp were scientists. They were rounded up because the Americans wanted their scientific expertise. And they wanted to keep that expertise from the Russians.
<v Karen Duffin>营地里的许多纳粹现在是科学家。他们被围捕是因为美国人想要他们的科学专长。他们想让那种专长远离俄国人。

00:50:35.260 --> 00:50:38.420
<v Karen Duffin>Arno was what they called a morale officer.
<v Karen Duffin>Arno是他们所谓的士气官。

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:41.330
<v Karen Duffin>So what were your instructions with these guys?
<v Karen Duffin>那么你对这些家伙的指示是什么？

00:50:41.330 --> 00:50:42.570
<v Arno Mayer>Keep them happy.
<v Arno Mayer>让他们快乐。

00:50:42.570 --> 00:50:49.750
<v Karen Duffin>Make the Nazis happy. Not defeat them, not get information out of them. Charm them, get them to join our team.
<v Karen Duffin>让纳粹快乐。不是打败他们，不是从他们那里获取信息。吸引他们，让他们加入我们的团队。

00:50:49.750 --> 00:50:58.370
<v Arno Mayer>It didn't make any sense. The whole thing didn't make any sense. I mean, the whole thing was so completely surreal, completely surreal.
<v Arno Mayer>这根本说不通。整件事根本说不通。我是说，整件事太超现实了，完全超现实。

00:50:58.370 --> 00:51:27.440
<v Karen Duffin>Arno was assigned to the most famous Nazi recruit, Wernher von Braun. He designed the V-2 rocket which was targeted at civilians in London. His rocket factory used Jewish slaves. But Arno says they never discussed that. Instead, he and the others entertained the scientists. Some played games with them like volleyball or chess, or brought them liquor and newspapers, took them on outings to DC. Like a concierge, but for Nazis.
<v Karen Duffin>Arno被指派给最著名的纳粹新兵，Wernher von Braun。他设计了针对伦敦平民的V-2火箭。他的火箭工厂使用了犹太奴隶。但Arno说他们从未讨论过那个。相反，他和其他人娱乐那些科学家。有些人通过玩像排球或象棋这样的游戏来娱乐他们，或者给他们带来酒和报纸，带他们去华盛顿DC郊游。就像礼宾部，但却是为纳粹服务的。

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:38.280
<v Karen Duffin>The scientists told Arno about their wives and their kids, their lives. And after all that, he said it was hard to see the Nazis as just monsters.
<v Karen Duffin>那些科学家告诉Arno关于他们的妻子和孩子，他们的生活。经过这一切，他说很难把纳粹仅仅视为怪物。

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:48.800
<v Arno Mayer>I don't know. I mean, you can't just then proceed on the assumption well, these are subhumans and to hell with them. And the next time you bring a bottle of wine you put poison in it, you know.
<v Arno Mayer>我不知道。我是说，你不能仅仅基于那种假设，好吧，这些是次人类，去死吧，然后接着做。下次你带一瓶酒时，你在里面下毒，你知道。

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:50.090
<v Karen Duffin>Really?
<v Karen Duffin>真的吗？

00:51:50.090 --> 00:51:55.050
<v Arno Mayer>I don't know. I don't know. It's a difficult question.
<v Arno Mayer>我不知道。我不知道。这是一个困难的问题。

00:51:55.050 --> 00:52:01.750
<v Karen Duffin>Over time, the indignities of the job piled up. Until a small comment from von Braun set Arno off.
<v Karen Duffin>随着时间的推移，工作的屈辱堆积起来。直到von Braun的一句小评论让Arno爆发。

00:52:01.750 --> 00:52:19.490
<v Arno Mayer>And he had already spoken behind my back of me as [GERMAN], a little Jew boy. So I lost it. And I stomped out. I was pretty shaken. Went back to my barrack and I'm summoned to the commander of the camp. Somebody had told him what I had said.
<v Arno Mayer>他已经在我背后称我为[德语]，一个小犹太男孩。所以我失去了控制。我气冲冲地走了。我非常震惊。回到我的营房，我被传唤到营地指挥官那里。有人告诉了他我说了什么。

00:52:19.490 --> 00:52:29.170
<v Arno Mayer>And finally he said, if  you do this again you'll be court-martialed. That was for me one of the turning points, really.
<v Arno Mayer>最后他说，如果你再这样做，你就会被送上军事法庭。对我来说，那是转折点之一，真的。

00:52:29.170 --> 00:52:38.740
<v Karen Duffin>After that, Arno became quietly subversive. Like when they asked to go shopping, Arno, for his own amusement, took them to a Jewish department store.
<v Karen Duffin>在那之后，Arno变得安静地颠覆。就像当他们要求购物时，Arno为了他自己的乐趣，把他们带到了一家犹太百货公司。

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:50.070
<v Arno Mayer>So he came and he said look, I'd like to send packages to our families in Germany, because it's going to be a rough winter. We'd like to go shopping. I was told, make them happy. So what the hell.
<v Arno Mayer>所以他来了，他说看，我想寄包裹给我们在德国的家人，因为那将是一个严酷的冬天。我们想去购物。我被告知，让他们快乐。所以管他呢。

00:52:50.070 --> 00:53:03.470
<v Arno Mayer>And I began to be amused by this and decided that I was going to take them to the biggest Jewish department store in Washington, DC. So in    the car I asked them, what do we start with? Well, we want to buy underwear for our wives.
<v Arno Mayer>我开始觉得这很有趣，并决定把他们带到华盛顿DC最大的犹太百货公司。在车里我问他们，我们从什么开始？好吧，我们想给我们的妻子买内衣。

00:53:03.470 --> 00:53:28.390
<v Arno Mayer>In those days, they didn't have bins in which this stuff was in. There was a sales lady. So the four of them, since they were scientists, you had these slide rules. They took them out as the woman asked what size, you see. So the woman came out and she held up a nylon panty. And Wernher von Braun said [GERMAN] but no, made of wool and with long legs.
<v Arno Mayer>在那些日子里，他们没有装这些东西的箱子。有一个女售货员。所以他们四个，因为他们是科学家，你有这些计算尺。当女人问尺码时，他们把它们拿出来了，你明白吗。所以女人出来了，她举起一条尼龙内裤。Wernher von Braun说[德语]，但不是，它是羊毛做的，有长腿。

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:43.090
<v Arno Mayer>In the meantime, people started looking around. There with these guys in these leather coats, and then there was this little guy next to them in an American uniform. And just as they were telling the lady about the sizes, the military police came and arrested the whole bunch of us.
<v Arno Mayer>与此同时，人们开始四处张望。那里有这些穿着皮大衣的家伙，然后旁边有这个穿着美国军装的小个子男人。就在他们告诉女人关于尺码的时候，宪兵来了，把我们一帮人都抓了。

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:49.980
<v Arno Mayer>[CHUCKLING]
<v Arno Mayer>[笑声]

00:53:49.980 --> 00:54:05.330
<v Karen Duffin>They were conspicuous, this group of German-looking men making a ruckus. Arno told the police he was on a secret military mission. They verified it and let him go. But Arno says he knows his rebellion was small. And his restraint still haunts him.
<v Karen Duffin>他们很显眼，这群德国长相的男人制造了骚动。Arno告诉警察他是在执行秘密军事任务。他们验证了它并让他走了。但Arno说他知道他的叛乱很小。而他的克制仍然困扰着他。

00:54:05.330 --> 00:54:12.520
<v Arno Mayer>I should have told them to go to hell. But I didn't do it. I was a coward. I mean, I only exploded once. I could have exploded many other times.
<v Arno Mayer>我本应该告诉他们去见鬼去吧。但我没做。我是个懦夫。我是说，我只爆发过一次。我本可以爆发更多次的。

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:13.010
<v Karen Duffin>Do you wish you had?
<v Karen Duffin>你希望你曾经爆发过吗？

00:54:13.010 --> 00:54:17.140
<v Arno Mayer>Yeah, you're god damn right, god damn right, as I look back.
<v Arno Mayer>是啊，该死的你是对的，该死的你是对的，当我回头看时。

00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:28.130
<v Karen Duffin>Wernher von Braun stayed in the United States and became something of an American hero. He helped us get to the moon. He was on the cover of Time. He became buddies with JFK.
<v Karen Duffin>Wernher von Braun留在了美国，成为了某种美国英雄。他帮助我们登上了月球。他登上了《时代》周刊的封面。他成了JFK的哥们。

00:54:28.130 --> 00:54:49.620
<v Karen Duffin>And PO Box 1142 closed down. Its buildings were bulldozed. The files were burned. And the men who worked there went on to become lawyers, a CIA agent, an ambassador, head of the Culinary Institute of America, conductor of the Chicago Chamber Orchestra, the richest man in America in the '80s, John Kluge.
<v Karen Duffin>PO Box 1142关闭了。它的建筑物被推土机推平了。文件被烧毁了。在那里工作的人后来成了律师、CIA特工、大使、美国烹饪学院院长、芝加哥室内乐团指挥、80年代美国首富John Kluge。

00:54:49.620 --> 00:55:21.850
<v Karen Duffin>Arno became a history professor at Princeton. And the guy who lost it and pissed on the Nazi started his own textile business. And all these men whose job it had been to extract secrets now carried their own. Most never said a word to their wives or kids about the work they had done, about the intimate war they had fought with the Germans in a park in Virginia.
<v Karen Duffin>Arno成了普林斯顿的历史教授。而那个失去控制并在纳粹身上撒尿的家伙创办了自己的纺织公司。所有这些曾经的工作是提取秘密的男人现在背负着他们自己的秘密。大多数人从未对他们的妻子或孩子说一句话关于他们做过的工作，关于他们在弗吉尼亚州公园与德国人进行的亲密战争。

00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:30.220
<v Ira Glass>Karen Duffin is one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>Karen Duffin是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:55:30.220 --> 00:55:38.130
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "WERNHER VON BRAUN" BY TOM LEHRER]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "WERNHER VON BRAUN" BY TOM LEHRER]

00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:55.530
<v Ira Glass>(SINGING) Gather 'round while I sing you of Wernher von Braun, a man whose allegiance is ruled by expedience. Call him a Nazi, he won't even for frown, Nazi, schmatzy, says Wernher von Braun.
<v Ira Glass>(演唱) 聚在一起，当我要为你唱关于Wernher von Braun，一个忠诚被权宜之计统治的人。叫他纳粹，他甚至不会皱眉，纳粹，没关系，Wernher von Braun说。

00:55:55.530 --> 00:56:12.610
<v Ira Glass>Don't say that he's hypocritical. Say rather that he's apolitical. Once the rockets are up, who cares where they come down. That's not my department, says Wernher von Braun.
<v Ira Glass>不要说他是虚伪的。倒不如说他是非政治的。火箭升空后，谁在乎它们会落到哪里。那不是我的部门，Wernher von Braun说。

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:27.500
<v Ira Glass>Some have harsh words for this man of renown. But some think our attitude should be one of gratitude. Like the widows and cripples in old London town, who owe their large pensions to Werner von Braun.
<v Ira Glass>有些人对这个有名的人有刻薄的评论。但有些人认为我们的态度应该是感恩。就像伦敦老城的寡妇和残疾人，他们欠Wernher von Braun的高额养老金。

00:56:27.500 --> 00:56:47.620
<v Ira Glass>You too may be a hero once you've learned to count backwards to zero. In German or English I know how to count down. And I'm learning Chinese says Wernher von Braun.
<v Ira Glass>你也可以成为英雄，一旦你学会从零开始倒数。用德语或英语我知道怎么倒数。而且我正在学习中文，Wernher von Braun说。

00:56:47.620 --> 00:57:01.100
<v Ira Glass>Our program is produced today by Susan Burton and David Kestenbaum. Our Production staff, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvas, Sean Cole, Neil Drumming, Karen Duffin, Emmanuel Dzotsi, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Alissa Ship, Matt Tierney, and Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由Susan Burton和David Kestenbaum制作。我们的制作人员，Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvas, Sean Cole, Neil Drumming, Karen Duffin, Emmanuel Dzotsi, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Alissa Ship, Matt Tierney, 和 Nancy Updike。

00:57:01.100 --> 00:57:04.980
<v Ira Glass>Our editor is Joel Lovell. Editing help from Julie Snyder and Elna Baker.
<v Ira Glass>我们的编辑是Joel Lovell。Julie Snyder和Elna Baker提供编辑帮助。

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:24.770
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:24.770 --> 00:57:48.880
<v Ira Glass>At our website this week we have all kinds of stuff. The fax that Jack Bailey received assigning him to represent Trastavien Hardy that we've annotated with all kinds of revealing information. Also pictures and documents about PO Box 1142, including stuff on their interrogation techniques. And something called-- no kidding-- the POW Coddling Report. That's at our website, ThisAmericanLife.org where you can also sign up for our weekly newsletter.
<v Ira Glass>本周在我们的网站上有各种各样的东西。Jack Bailey收到的指派他代表Trastavien Hardy的传真，我们标注了各种揭示信息。还有关于PO Box 1142的照片和文件，包括关于他们审问技术的东西。还有一些叫作——没开玩笑——“战俘溺爱报告”的东西。那在我们的网站，ThisAmericanLife.org，你也可以在那里订阅我们的每周通讯。

00:57:48.880 --> 00:57:53.680
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由PRX，公共广播交流中心交付给公共广播电台。

00:57:53.680 --> 00:58:01.120
<v Ira Glass>Thanks as always to our program's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia who, ever since we've met, he's had this affectionate nickname I've always liked that he's always liked to call me--
<v Ira Glass>一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人Torey Malatia先生，他自我们见面以来，一直有一个我一直很喜欢的昵称，他一直喜欢称呼我——

00:58:01.120 --> 00:58:03.870
<v Arno Mayer>[GERMAN], a little Jew boy.
<v Arno Mayer>[德语]，一个小犹太男孩。

00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:08.870
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是Ira Glass。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
